From d64c220f49be0e26e18f7bf707c4e6a9d5ca5fa8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tm <tm@x>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 19:40:52 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 34/36] Flat closures: a closure keeps the values of the
 variables it uses, the variables captured and set! are boxed

From Julia's femtolisp (f21780ccb0, d323f404c9). A closure kept the
whole frame of the function which made it: at the first closure, the
frame moved to a vector of the heap and every access to an argument of
that function then went through the vector (the flag "captured" of each
frame), and loadc walked the chain of the frames. Now:
- the compiler finds the variables of a lambda which are both captured
  by a closure and set! (complex-bindings), and the instruction box puts
  each in a pair, whose car is the value;
- a closure is the function with a vector of the values of the free
  variables it uses (a box for those): closure n takes them from the
  stack, loadc i reads the vector, loadc0 and loadc1 are the short forms;
- loadc/setc lose their depth operand, setc and setc.l are gone (a
  captured variable which is set! is a box), and the frames lose the
  captured slot: 4 slots instead of 5 (process_keys, vargc, optargs,
  gc, _stacktrace follow).
Arguments are always on the stack, a closure is made from its free
variables only, and the frames which make closures are no longer copied
to the heap.

TeXmacs adaptations:
- resolve-global (0003) applies to the global names read and set!;
- the env given to compile-unknown-call (0010) is still the list of the
  frames of names (env-names), as the env of the compiler is now made of
  (name boxed? index);
- the body of trycatch is a closure (a thunk) for complex-bindings, so
  that a variable set! there and used after it is boxed;
- the default values of the optional and keyword arguments, of the
  function and of the lambdas inside it, are part of the analysis (as if they were closures: Julia's femtolisp ignored them,
  a closure made there which set! an argument was an internal error), and
  an argument is boxed as soon as it has its value (the required ones
  before the default values, an optional one after its default value);
  a closure made in a default value and an argument set! later share the
  box (with the old closures, the closure kept the frame before the
  keyword arguments were sorted and saw the wrong values);
- set! of a boxed variable gives the value, not the box (set-car! then
  car);
- a function which captures more than 255 variables is a compile error
  (the operand of closure has 8 bits);
- function:env of a closure is the vector of the captured values (it was
  the vector of the frame, the next frame as its last element);
- in the stack traces, a boxed argument is shown as its box, a list of
  one element.
Boot image regenerated with the cross step of the previous patch.
